// ======================================================================= 7. the Revelation Engine (docs/research/revelation-engine.md)
// Each step is a discovery loop: predict (with how sure you are), then see, then find the edge of the rule, then meet it
// again later in a new situation. Numbers: P(ill | +) = .01·.9 / (.01·.9 + .99·.09) ≈ 9.2 %, after a second independent
// positive ≈ 50.3 %; spam .1·.99 / (.1·.99 + .9·.02) ≈ 84.6 %. Schelling at t = 34 %: like neighbours ≈ 50 % → ≈ 75 %
// (7 seeds, 24 × 24, 88 % full). Heater (max power 3, leak 0.1/s): gain 0.8, delay 1 s settles near 19.8 °C with no
// overshoot; gain 3, delay 6 s peaks near 29 °C and crosses the setpoint 5 times in a minute.
const REV = part('Discovery: predict, then see', 'Four ideas you uncover by predicting first: evidence and belief, a pattern nobody chose, a loop that overshoots, and an analogy with its seams.', 'What changes when you guess before you are told?',
  [['7.1', 'Evidence and belief'], ['7.2', 'Emergence'], ['7.3', 'Feedback control'], ['7.4', 'Analogy']], [
  { title: 'A positive test for a rare illness', section: '7.1', visual: { domain: 'data', structure: 'inference', primitive: 'bayes_update' },
    statement: 'How much a piece of evidence should move a belief depends on how likely that evidence is under each explanation and on how common each explanation was to begin with (Bayes\' rule).',
    explain: [{ text: 'One person in a hundred has the illness. The test catches 90 % of people who have it, and wrongly flags 9 % of people who do not. Out of 1000 people, 10 are ill and 9 of them test positive; 990 are well and about 89 of them test positive too. So of the 98 positives only 9 are ill: about 9 %, not 90 %. A second, independent positive multiplies the odds again by the same factor of ten, to about one in two.',
      figure: { kind: 'bayes_update', caption: 'Reveal the evidence one card at a time. Before each, guess which way the bars will move.', question: 'Is this person ill?',
        hypotheses: [{ id: 'ill', label: 'has the illness', prior: .01 }, { id: 'well', label: 'does not', prior: .99 }],
        evidence: [{ id: 't1', label: 'Test 1 positive', like: { ill: .9, well: .09 }, note: 'Ten times likelier if ill, but there are 99 well people for every ill one.' }, { id: 't2', label: 'Test 2 positive (independent)', like: { ill: .9, well: .09 }, note: 'Independent errors multiply: the odds rise ten-fold again.' }] } }],
    terms: [{ term: 'prior', meaning: 'how likely an explanation is before the evidence' }, { term: 'likelihood', meaning: 'how likely the evidence is if the explanation is true' }],
    misconception: 'Reading "90 % accurate" as "90 % chance I am ill". The test\'s accuracy says how likely a positive is if you are ill; the question is how likely you are ill given a positive, and that depends on how rare the illness is.',
    reveal: { type: 'quantitative', hook: 'The illness affects 1 in 100 people. The test catches 90 % of cases and wrongly flags 9 % of healthy people. Someone tests positive. How likely is it that they are ill?',
      predict: { options: [{ text: 'About 90 %: the test is 90 % accurate.', why: 'That is the chance of a positive if ill, not of being ill if positive.' }, { text: 'About 50 %.', why: 'That is where a second independent positive takes it.' }, { text: 'About 9 %.', correct: true, why: '9 ill positives among about 98 positives in every 1000 people.' }] },
      rule: 'Posterior odds = prior odds × how many times likelier the evidence is under one explanation than the other.',
      boundary: { case: 'Only when the tests\' errors are independent: a second test that shares the first one\'s cause of false alarms (the same lab, the same cross-reacting antibody) moves belief much less than ten-fold.', why: 'Multiplying likelihoods assumes each piece of evidence is new information.' },
      transfer: { q: 'A spam filter flags 99 % of spam and 2 % of genuine mail; one email in ten is spam. A flagged email is spam with probability about…', options: [{ text: '99 %' }, { text: '85 %', correct: true }, { text: '50 %' }], why: 'Of 1000 emails, 99 spam are flagged and 18 genuine ones: 99 / 117 ≈ 85 %.' } },
    sources: [SRC.gigerenzer, SRC.mcdowell], opens: 'Can a pattern appear that no single person chose?' },
  { title: 'A town that sorts itself', section: '7.2', visual: { domain: 'economics', structure: 'emergence', primitive: 'emergence' },
    statement: 'Agents who only mind their immediate neighbours can, together, produce a city-wide pattern far stronger than any of them wanted: a mild local preference is enough for strong segregation (Schelling 1971).',
    explain: [{ text: 'Two kinds of resident, scattered at random, so about half of anyone\'s neighbours are like them. Each is content as long as at least a third of its neighbours are like itself, so each would happily live as a minority. An unhappy one moves to a random empty spot. Nobody wants a sorted town, yet after a few rounds about three-quarters of everyone\'s neighbours are alike.',
      figure: { kind: 'emergence', caption: 'Hollow shapes are unhappy. Run it, then compare preferences of 25 % and 60 %.', threshold: .34, size: 24, density: .88, share: .5, seed: 7 } }],
    misconception: 'That a strongly sorted pattern must come from strong preferences. Here every agent would accept being outnumbered two to one.',
    reveal: { type: 'causal', hook: 'Each resident only wants at least a third of its neighbours to be like itself. Starting from a random town (about half alike), how alike will neighbourhoods end up?',
      predict: { options: [{ text: 'About the same, around 50 %: nobody wants more than a third.', why: 'Each move disturbs other neighbourhoods and triggers more moves.' }, { text: 'A bit more, around 60 %.' }, { text: 'Strongly sorted, around 75 %.', correct: true, why: 'Seven runs of this town end between 71 % and 78 %.' }] },
      rule: 'Local rules can add up to a global pattern none of the agents intended, and much stronger than their own preferences.',
      boundary: { case: 'The model shows that mild preferences are enough to sort a town, not that they are what sorted any real one: prices, laws, lending and history act too, and the model leaves them out.', why: 'Sufficient is not the same as actual.' },
      transfer: { q: 'Drivers on a busy ring road each brake a little when the car ahead gets close. With no accident or bottleneck, what can appear?', options: [{ text: 'Nothing: small braking averages out.' }, { text: 'Stop-and-go waves that travel backwards along the road.', correct: true }, { text: 'Perfectly even spacing.' }], why: 'Each driver over-reacts slightly to the one ahead; the delay amplifies down the line into a jam no driver caused (phantom traffic jams).' } },
    sources: [SRC.schelling], opens: 'What happens when a system corrects itself, but late?' },
  { title: 'A thermostat that answers late', section: '7.3', visual: { domain: 'physical', structure: 'control', primitive: 'control_loop' },
    statement: 'A feedback controller pushes in proportion to the error between what it wants and what it measures; when the measurement arrives late, a strong push overshoots and the system rings around its goal.',
    explain: [{ text: 'The heater is set from the gap between the setpoint and the measured temperature. With a quick sensor and a gentle gain the room creeps up and settles, a little below the setpoint, because the room keeps leaking heat and only a gap keeps the heater on. Make the sensor six seconds late and the gain strong: the heater is still pushing hard when the room has already passed the setpoint, so it overshoots, cools, undershoots, and rings.',
      figure: { kind: 'control_loop', caption: 'Move the gain and the delay; open a window halfway to disturb it.', setpoint: 21, start: 15, gain: .8, delay: 1, leak: .1, ambient: 10, unit: '°C', output: 'room temperature', actuator: 'heater power' } }],
    terms: [{ term: 'setpoint', meaning: 'the value the controller tries to reach' }, { term: 'gain', meaning: 'how hard the controller pushes per degree of error' }],
    misconception: 'That a stronger controller always reaches the goal faster. With a delay in the loop, a stronger one overshoots and oscillates.',
    reveal: { type: 'causal', hook: 'The sensor now reports 6 seconds late. You turn the gain up from 0.8 to 3. What does the room temperature do?',
      predict: { options: [{ text: 'Reaches 21 °C faster and stays there.', why: 'It reaches it faster, then keeps going: the heater learns it is too warm 6 s late.' }, { text: 'Overshoots to the high twenties and swings around 21 °C.', correct: true, why: 'Peak about 29 °C, five crossings of the setpoint in a minute.' }, { text: 'Never warms up.' }] },
      rule: 'Delay plus strong correction makes a loop overshoot and oscillate; a loop with delay needs a gentler push.',
      boundary: { case: 'Even with no delay, a purely proportional controller fighting a leak settles a little below the setpoint (here about 19.8 °C), because only a remaining gap keeps the heater on.', why: 'Removing that offset takes integral action, which brings its own risk of overshoot.' },
      transfer: { q: 'In a shower, the hot water takes five seconds to reach you. Each time it feels cold you turn the tap a lot towards hot. What happens?', options: [{ text: 'You quickly get the right temperature.' }, { text: 'You swing between too hot and too cold.', correct: true }, { text: 'It stays cold.' }], why: 'The same loop: a strong correction acting on stale information overshoots both ways.' } },
    sources: [], opens: 'Can a familiar picture help, if you know where it fails?' },
  { title: 'Electric current, pictured as water', section: '7.4', visual: { domain: 'physical', structure: 'analogy', primitive: 'analogy_map' },
    statement: 'In a simple circuit, voltage behaves like a pressure difference, current like a flow rate and resistance like a narrow pipe, so two equal resistances in series pass about half the current of one; the picture fails for what leaves a cut, how fast charges move, and alternating current.',
    explain: [{ text: 'A pump lifts water and pushes it round a loop of pipe; a narrow section resists the flow, and two narrow sections one after the other resist twice as much. A battery does the same for charge: it keeps a voltage between its ends, and resistances in series add, so the current falls. Where the picture is wrong matters as much as where it is right.',
      figure: { kind: 'analogy_map', caption: 'Click a row to follow one correspondence; the torn edge is where the analogy stops.', source: 'water in pipes', target: 'electric circuit',
        maps: [['pump', 'battery', 'keeps a difference'], ['pressure difference', 'voltage', 'drives the flow'], ['flow rate (litres/s)', 'current (coulombs/s)', 'what moves per second'], ['narrow pipe', 'resistor', 'resists the flow'], ['two narrow pipes in a row', 'two resistors in series', 'resistances add']],
        breaks: ['A cut pipe spills water; a cut wire stops the current, and no charge pours out.', 'Water in a pipe moves at the flow speed; electrons drift at millimetres a second while the signal travels at a large fraction of light speed.', 'Alternating current, where charges rock back and forth, has no counterpart in steady pipe flow.'] } }],
    misconception: 'That current is "used up" by the first bulb, so the second gets less. The same current flows through both bulbs in series; it is the voltage that is shared.',
    reveal: { type: 'causal', hook: 'Two identical bulbs are connected one after the other to a battery. Compared with one bulb alone, the current is about…',
      predict: { options: [{ text: 'The same: each bulb takes what it needs.' }, { text: 'Half, and both bulbs carry that same current.', correct: true, why: 'Two narrow pipes in a row: twice the resistance, half the flow, and the flow is the same all the way round.' }, { text: 'The first bulb gets most of it, the second the rest.', why: 'Charge is not used up along the way.' }] },
      rule: 'Resistances in series add, so the current falls, and the same current flows through every part of a single loop.',
      boundary: { case: 'A real bulb\'s filament resists more when hot; two dimmer bulbs run cooler, so the current is somewhat more than half.', why: '"Half" holds exactly only for resistances that do not change with temperature.' },
      transfer: { q: 'Two identical heating elements are put in series on the same supply instead of one alone. The total heat given off is…', options: [{ text: 'Twice as much.' }, { text: 'The same.' }, { text: 'Half as much.', correct: true }], why: 'Half the current through twice the resistance: power I²R falls to (½)²·2 = ½.' } },
    sources: [SRC.gentner, SRC.clement], opens: 'Which of your own ideas would you test by predicting first?' }]);
